## Withdrawal and Rollover Rules: SIMPLE IRA vs 401(k)

According to the [IRS](https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/retirement-topics-tax-on-early-distributions), taking money out of a SIMPLE IRA within your first two years of participation costs a 25% additional tax instead of the usual 10% penalty, while early distributions from a 401(k) face the standard 10% penalty alongside an age-55 separation exception. Both retirement plans generally require you to pay regular income tax on pre-tax withdrawals taken before age 59½. That steep 25% early withdrawal tax makes pulling money out of a newer SIMPLE IRA far more expensive than tapping a 401(k).

The two-year clock begins on the exact day your employer deposits the first contribution into your SIMPLE IRA. During this initial two-year window, you can only roll over balances tax-free from one SIMPLE IRA to another SIMPLE IRA. The IRS treats any rollover to a 401(k) or a Traditional IRA during this period as an early distribution, which triggers ordinary income tax and the full 25% penalty.

Once that two-year period ends, rollover restrictions loosen and the penalty drops to the standard 10% rate. After two years of participation, the IRS allows you to roll over SIMPLE IRA balances tax-free into a 401(k), a Traditional IRA, or another eligible workplace plan. You can also move those funds into a Roth IRA after two years, though you must pay income taxes on any pre-tax dollars you convert.

The age-55 separation rule marks another major distinction, allowing penalty-free distributions from a 401(k) if you leave or lose your job in the calendar year you turn 55 or older. That rule applies only to the 401(k) held with the employer you just left, and the IRS does not extend this exception to a SIMPLE IRA or any other IRA. Standard penalty exceptions, including total and permanent disability, death, and qualified military reservist distributions, apply equally to both plans.

For a closer look at how workplace plans differ from an individual account you manage entirely on your own, review our [Traditional IRA vs. SIMPLE IRA comparison](/compare/traditional-ira-vs-simple-ira/). That comparison explains how an individual account avoids the mandatory employer contribution structures and the two-year 25% penalty window while offering a wider investment menu.

Neither plan serves as an effective holding place for money you might need before age 59½, and most employees cannot choose between them because the employer decides which plan to sponsor. For business owners and savers deciding how much to defer, plan selection still hinges on headcount limits, required employer contributions, and administrative costs covered across this guide. A direct change in IRS penalty codes or federal distribution limits would be the only development that changes this advice.